WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.500
Part 3. You will hear two education students discussing an assignment on teaching handwriting to children.

00:00:08.500 --> 00:00:14.500
First, you have some time to look at questions 21 to 24.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:47.000
Now listen carefully and answer questions 21 to 24.

00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:50.500
How are you getting on with the assignment on handwriting?

00:00:50.500 --> 00:00:58.500
Not too bad. You know, I hadn't realised that children benefit in so many ways from learning to write.

00:00:58.500 --> 00:01:05.500
It's such an important skill and yet most people think handwriting is less important than in the past

00:01:05.500 --> 00:01:08.500
because people hardly ever write by hand these days.

00:01:08.500 --> 00:01:15.500
Yes, and all the evidence suggests children should learn to write by hand before they learn to type,

00:01:15.500 --> 00:01:17.500
not least because it helps their memory.

00:01:17.500 --> 00:01:23.500
That's right. The physical act of writing helps children to remember letters.

00:01:23.500 --> 00:01:26.500
That seems pretty obvious when you think about it.

00:01:26.500 --> 00:01:30.500
What's less obvious is how it helps develop their concentration.

00:01:30.500 --> 00:01:34.500
They have to sit still and focus on one thing.

00:01:34.500 --> 00:01:38.500
Yeah, that aspect of handwriting had never occurred to me before.

00:01:38.500 --> 00:01:44.500
Same here. I'm not sure I understand how it improves children's imagination though.

00:01:44.500 --> 00:01:48.500
Well, there was that study which showed that primary age children

00:01:48.500 --> 00:01:53.500
generated more ideas when they were writing by hand than using a keyboard.

00:01:53.500 --> 00:01:56.500
I would have guessed that would be the case.

00:01:56.500 --> 00:02:02.500
Hmm, yeah. I'd never associated spatial awareness with handwriting either.

00:02:02.500 --> 00:02:09.500
I thought spatial awareness was more to do with knowing where you are in relation to objects or other people.

00:02:09.500 --> 00:02:11.500
Mmm, I thought that too.

00:02:11.500 --> 00:02:18.500
But good spatial awareness is essential for writing because you have to space words correctly.

00:02:18.500 --> 00:02:24.500
It's not just fine motor skills that improve through writing as I'd always assumed.

00:02:24.500 --> 00:02:31.500
Handwriting is so much harder for children with dyspraxia who have problems coordinating movement.

00:02:31.500 --> 00:02:35.500
It's good there are lots of things you can do in the classroom to help them.

00:02:35.500 --> 00:02:38.500
They need so much more support with letter formation.

00:02:38.500 --> 00:02:42.500
You need to play lots of games to help them distinguish letter shapes.

00:02:42.500 --> 00:02:44.500
It takes a lot of patience.

00:02:44.500 --> 00:02:50.500
Yeah, I like the idea of using one of those pens that lights up if you press too hard.

00:02:50.500 --> 00:02:53.500
That seems like a really simple solution.

00:02:53.500 --> 00:03:00.500
Yes, absolutely. I'm not sure there's much you can do about children with dyspraxia writing very slowly.

00:03:00.500 --> 00:03:08.500
It's more important to focus on accuracy and as they get more confident, I think they eventually speed up.

00:03:08.500 --> 00:03:12.500
One quite simple thing you can do is to use grid paper.

00:03:12.500 --> 00:03:18.500
So they write each letter in a box and that trains them to space the letters correctly.

00:03:18.500 --> 00:03:24.500
Indeed, that's more important for legibility than trying to get them to write in a straight line.

00:03:24.500 --> 00:03:34.500
Before you hear the rest of the discussion, you have some time to look at questions 25 to 30.

00:03:54.500 --> 00:03:57.500
Thank you.

00:04:24.500 --> 00:04:28.500
Now listen and answer questions 25 to 30.

00:04:32.500 --> 00:04:40.500
For some children, it might be better to teach them to write on a laptop rather than by hand, like children with dyslexia.

00:04:40.500 --> 00:04:45.500
They often really struggle with handwriting and some just give up.

00:04:45.500 --> 00:04:49.500
Yeah, it's not as frustrating for them if they get things wrong.

00:04:49.500 --> 00:04:53.500
On a keyboard, they can be more willing to have a go.

00:04:53.500 --> 00:04:57.500
But I read that developing fluency isn't any faster.

00:04:57.500 --> 00:05:06.500
That's right. Did you read that article on the benefits of teaching print rather than cursive handwriting, where the letters are joined up?

00:05:06.500 --> 00:05:15.500
Yes. Well, in the past, cursive writing was certainly considered more stylish and educated, but not anymore.

00:05:15.500 --> 00:05:21.500
Teachers' attitudes have changed because it's been proved that cursive is more difficult to learn,

00:05:21.500 --> 00:05:27.500
especially for children with learning difficulties who find joining up letters really challenging.

00:05:27.500 --> 00:05:37.500
I agree. I was always worried that my poor handwriting affected my exam results, and now research shows that I was right to worry.

00:05:37.500 --> 00:05:44.500
I'm sure a lot of students think it's unfair that they're being judged on their handwriting, not just their knowledge.

00:05:44.500 --> 00:05:48.500
Marks are definitely affected if examiners can't read the script.

00:05:48.500 --> 00:05:53.500
That's why it's always been so important to teach children to write legibly.

00:05:53.500 --> 00:05:57.500
Do you think the role of handwriting will change in the future?

00:05:57.500 --> 00:06:05.500
I can't see that changing much. Touch typing still isn't taught in most schools, which is a shame.

00:06:05.500 --> 00:06:13.500
But maybe that won't be necessary in the future because people will also be able to write by hand on digital devices.

00:06:13.500 --> 00:06:19.500
Anyway, teachers understand the value of handwriting. It's a basic life skill.

00:06:19.500 --> 00:06:28.500
True. However, the fact is that people are writing by hand less and less and relying on digital devices.

00:06:28.500 --> 00:06:30.500
That does cause some problems.

00:06:30.500 --> 00:06:35.500
You mean like note-taking? There are lots of apps for that.

00:06:35.500 --> 00:06:38.500
And for reading historical documents, apparently.

00:06:38.500 --> 00:06:45.500
But my mum is shocked by my awful spelling and the fact that my punctuation is really inconsistent.

00:06:45.500 --> 00:06:48.500
I think you can put that down to lack of practice.

00:06:48.500 --> 00:06:55.500
I expect so. Personally, I miss writing by hand. I hardly ever write anything now.

00:06:55.500 --> 00:07:01.500
I remember my grandparents had such beautiful handwriting and it was so individual.

00:07:01.500 --> 00:07:06.500
Nobody I know would be able to identify my handwriting now. It's a shame.

00:07:06.500 --> 00:07:14.500
I know. I feel the same way. I used to write a diary by hand and now I do that digitally.

00:07:14.500 --> 00:07:20.500
It just seems less effort to do it that way. So it's not just a problem...

00:07:23.500 --> 00:07:31.500
That is the end of Part 3. You now have 30 seconds to check your answers to Part 3.

00:07:36.500 --> 00:07:38.500
Thank you.

